cartridge "VIDEO JIGSAW/COMPUTE!S GAZETTE/1986"

' VIDEO JIGSAW
' A ColecoVision game based on "Video Jigsaw" by Fred Karg, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, September 1986, pp. 65 and 107 (Commodore 64, BASIC + a small
' machine-language swap routine; disk 1986-09 file "video jigsaw", orig/).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a picture is cut into rectangular pieces and mixed up. Move the
' flashing arrow to a piece with the stick and press the left button to
' pick it up (corner marks show it), move the arrow to where it belongs and
' press the left button again: the two pieces swap places. The right button
' shows the finished picture; press a button to go back to the puzzle. Put
' every piece in its place to win. Moves and time are counted; the best
' result is kept for each picture (more pieces is better, then less time).
'
' From the original (orig/video jigsaw.bas): the player chooses the size of
' the pieces in character cells (the listing asks LENGTH 2-12 rows, then
' WIDTH 2-20 columns; the article says width 2-12 and length 2-20), from 4
' very large pieces (12 x 20) to 240 (2 x 2); the grid is as many whole
' pieces as fit (INT(40/W) x INT(25/L)) from the top left, the cells left
' over at the right and bottom stay in place; the pieces are shuffled with
' a random permutation swapped in pairs; a flashing arrow sprite at the
' top-left corner of a piece moves one piece at a time with the joystick;
' the button picks a piece (a low beep), and a second press elsewhere swaps
' the two (a higher beep); F1 shows the finished picture and F1 returns;
' when every piece is in place a fanfare plays and the player is asked to
' play again.
'
' Liberties: no picture files on the ColecoVision: four original built-in
' pictures (castle on a hill, lighthouse, hot-air balloon, sailboat) drawn
' as graphics-mode-2 tile art, 28 x 20 tiles at columns 2-29, rows 2-21;
' piece sizes in whole tiles, width 2-14 and length 2-10 (from 4 pieces,
' 14 x 10, to 140, 2 x 2), chosen with the stick on a setup screen that
' shows the picture; the arrow flashes at 2.5 Hz (the C64's toggled every
' BASIC pass, much faster); the stick repeats when held; picking the same
' piece again puts it back (the C64 kept it picked); the right button
' shows the picture (F1); a puzzle counts as solved when it LOOKS solved
' (pieces with identical tiles are interchangeable, as on a real jigsaw's
' sky they are not, but on screen nobody could tell); the finished picture
' is shown for two seconds before the shuffle; moves and time are counted
' and the best per picture is kept (none on the C64); no play-again
' question: a button returns to the title.
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick moves the arrow (and the menus), the
' left button picks and places, the right button shows the picture. On
' the title, up and down choose (START GAME, HOW TO PLAY, CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128 (each
' picture's 128 tiles are loaded into codes 128-255 of every screen
' third); the frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens are drawn with
' the NMI off after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table,
' effects after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is
' always given. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

